{ Your rook is deep in his position, right where his rook can grab it. But something much better is waiting. } 32. Rxb2 { His rook snaps up yours, and he is sure you will simply take his rook back. } Qe1+ { Your queen steps in front of his king and offers herself for nothing. Only the rook on his back row can take her, and take her he must. [%cal Gd1e1] } (32... Qxb2 { You take the rook back, which is exactly what he wanted. Queens and rooks stay level and his king is never in danger. } 33. Qxd3 Qb4 34. Qb1 Qxa4 { A queen and a rook each, the pawns level too, and the mate that was there for one move has gone for good. }) (32... d2 { Your pawn is one square from a new queen, and that is why the rook that just ate yours simply takes it. } 33. Rbxd2 Qe1+ 34. Qf1 Qxf1+ 35. Rxf1 { The queens come off, your brave pawn is gone, and you are a whole rook down with nothing left to attack. }) 33. Rxe1 { He has to take your queen. His king cannot move a square and nothing else of his can block the check. } Rxe1# { Mate! Your rook takes the rook that ate your queen, right on his back row, and his own two pawns seal the way out. That is a back rank mate. [%csl Rh1,Rg2,Rh2] } (33... Rd8 { Your rook slides away instead of taking, so his king lives. You paid a whole queen for a mate you did not play. } 34. Qc7 Rg8 35. Qd6 { A queen and a rook down, and his queen has come round to hunt your king in the corner. }) (33... Rf8 { Your rook steps aside to a quiet square while mate was one move away, and he gets to keep everything. } 34. Qxd3 Kg8 35. Rb7 { He picks off your advanced pawn and brings his rook up. You are a queen and a rook down with no attack at all. }) 0-1


