{ Now the smothered mate: his own pieces crowd so close around his king that a knight check leaves him no square at all. } 20... Kh8 { Your knight already had him in check, and the corner was the only square left. Now offer your queen right next to his king. [%cal Ye6g8] } 21. Qg8+ { Your queen steps beside his king, guarded by your knight. His rook has to take her, and that plugs the red square, his last way out. [%csl Rg8] } (21. Nf7+ { The knight check is the right idea in the wrong order: his rook simply takes your knight, and the smothered mate disappears. } 21... Rxf7 22. Qxf7 Qxa1 23. Nd2 Qe5+ { His queen has eaten your rook and now checks your king as well. You are a knight down and the mate is long gone. }) (21. Qe7 { A strong looking square, but your queen stops guarding the corner and his queen helps herself to your rook. } 21... Qxa1 22. Nf7+ Rxf7 23. Qxf7 Qxb1 { Both your knights and your rook are gone, and all you took was one rook. He is a whole rook ahead and his king is out of danger. }) (21. Qxa6 { Your queen wanders off to nibble a pawn, and his rook simply takes her. One pawn is not worth a queen. } 21... Rxa6 22. Ng4 Qxa1 23. Nd2 Rxa2 { Your queen and your rook are both gone and his pieces have marched into your corner. That one free pawn cost you the whole game. }) 21... Rxg8 { That was his only legal move, so the corner is now sealed by his own rook. Bring your knight in for the check. [%cal Yh6f7] } 22. Nf7# { Mate! Every square around his king is filled by his own rook and his own pawns, so one knight check finishes it. That is the smothered mate. [%csl Rh8,Gg8,Gg7,Gh7] } (22. Nxg8 { Your knight grabs the rook instead of mating. The material is level again, but his king simply walks out of the corner. } 22... Qxa1 23. f4 Qxb1 24. Nf6 gxf6 { Every piece you had is gone and his queen owns the board. Giving your queen away only works if the mate follows it. }) (22. Nf5 { Nothing was attacking your knight, so this saves a piece that was never in danger and lets the mate slip away. } 22... Qxa1 23. Nd2 Qe5+ 24. Ng3 Qf4 { You gave your queen and took nothing for it. His queen and both rooks are still standing, and now your king is the one in trouble. }) 1-0


