[Role "teaching"]

{ Now the ladder mate: your two rooks take turns checking, and every check pushes his king one step nearer the edge. [%cal Ye1g1] } 1. Rg1+ { Your rook checks down the column he is standing on, and your other rook holds the column behind him, so his only way out is the edge. [%csl Gf2] } (1. Rf4+ { Your rook steps right beside his king with nothing defending it, so his king simply eats it. } 1... Kxf4 2. Kg2 Kf5 3. Kf3 { A free rook for him. Your last rook can still force mate, but your king has to walk all the way over to help. }) (1. Rg2+ { A check, but the rook stops too close: his king steps beside it and attacks it, so you have to spend a move defending. } 1... Kf3 2. Kh2 Kf4 3. Rf2+ { You spent two moves saving that rook instead of checking, and his king is no nearer the edge than when you began. }) (1. Re4+ { Also a check and also a win, but this one chases him off a row, and then your rook has to come all the way back to check him on a column. } 1... Kg5 2. Re1 Kg4 3. Rg1+ { Your rook has come home and his king has come back, and you are playing the very check that was there two moves ago. }) 1... Kh3 { He is on the edge column with nothing behind him. Swing your other rook up beside him, onto a square your own king guards. [%cal Yf2h2] } 2. Rh2# { Mate! Your king guards the rook beside him, and your other rook covers the column he wanted to run to. That is the ladder mate. [%csl Rh3,Gh1,Gg1] } (2. Rg3+ { Careful: his king is standing right beside that square and nothing of yours defends a rook there. } 2... Kxg3 3. Rf8 Kg4 4. Kh2 { A whole rook thrown away one move before the mate. One rook can still finish the job, but it takes many more moves. }) (2. Rf3+ { This check comes along a row, so his king simply steps back a row and out of it. The mate was already sitting there. } 2... Kh4 3. Rg7 Kh5 4. Rh3# { The ladder catches him anyway, two moves later than it needed to. Same mate, just slower. }) 1-0


