{ The king and queen mate: your queen does the trapping, but she cannot finish alone, so your king comes to help. Walk her to the seventh row. [%cal Yb1b7] } 1. Qb7 { Your queen takes the whole seventh row away, so his king is shut on the last row with only two squares left. [%csl Rf8,Rh8] } (1. Qh7+ { Your queen lands right beside his king with nobody guarding her, and a king is allowed to take. } 1... Kxh7 { He eats your queen, and a king on his own can never mate anybody. The game is a draw. [%csl Rh7] }) (1. Qb8+ { A check along the last row, but his king simply walks out towards the edge instead of being trapped on it. } 1... Kh7 2. Qb7+ Kh6 3. Qh1# { Mate anyway, one move later than you needed: your queen seals the column and your king holds the three squares beside his king. }) (1. Qb3+ { A check from far away, and his king runs into the corner, where you have to trap him all over again. } 1... Kh8 2. Qb7 Kg8 3. Qg7# { Mate, but it took three moves. Taking the seventh row straight away did the same job faster. }) (1. Qg6+ { Also strong. Your queen checks along the column his king stands on, and he has to step across to the other side. } 1... Kf8 2. Qf7# { Mate! Your king guards your queen, and from right beside his king she covers every square he wanted. }) (1. Qa2+ { A check from the far corner, and his king hides in the other corner. You still have all the work in front of you. } 1... Kh8 2. Qa7 Kg8 3. Qg7# { Mate in the end, three moves instead of two. Your king is standing behind your queen so his king cannot touch her. }) (1. Qg1+ { Your queen checks along the column his king stands on, but he just steps off that column and nothing has been taken away from him. } 1... Kf8 2. Qg7+ Ke8 3. Qe7# { Mate all the same, two moves later than it needed to be. Your king is what stops him eating your queen. }) 1... Kf8 { He steps across, and he still has two squares. Now put your queen right beside his king, where your own king is guarding her. [%cal Yb7f7][%csl Gf6] } 2. Qf7# { Mate! Your queen stands right next to his king and your king guards her, so taking her is not allowed. That is the king and queen mate. [%csl Gf6,Rf8] } (2. Qb8# { Mate as well! Your queen owns the whole last row from far away, while your king holds the three squares in front of his king. [%csl Gf6] }) (2. Qa8# { Mate from the far corner. His king cannot step to either side of the last row, because your queen is watching all of it. }) (2. Qc8# { Mate again! Your king has taken the three squares in front of his king, so any check along the last row finishes the job. }) (2. Qe7+ { A check that shoves him across instead of finishing him, because your queen does not cover the square he runs to. } 2... Kg8 3. Qg7# { Mate, one move slower. Your queen is beside his king with your own king standing behind her. }) (2. Qg7+ { So close. Your queen is beside his king and guarded, but she leaves him one square on the other side, so it is only a check. } 2... Ke8 3. Qe7# { Mate at last. Your queen sits beside his king again with your king behind her, but it cost you a whole extra move. }) (2. Qb4+ { A check along the diagonal, and his king slides into the corner. Now your queen has to walk all the way over to him. } 2... Kg8 3. Qh4 Kf8 4. Qh8# { Mate, two extra moves later. Your king holds the squares in front of his king while your queen checks along the last row. }) 1-0


