{ His king is jammed in the corner and your king is close. Mate in one, but leave him with no move and no check and that is stalemate, a draw. } 1. Qd8# { Mate! Your king covers both green squares beside his king and your queen takes the whole last row. That is the king and queen mate. [%csl Ga7,Gb7,Rd8] } (1. Qd6 { Look at his king: not in check, and not one square to move to. That is stalemate, so the whole game ends as a draw. [%csl Ra8] }) (1. Qd5+ { A check along the diagonal, but it leaves him the square out of the corner, so he walks free instead of being mated. } 1... Kb8 2. Qb7# { Mate one move later. Your queen is beside his king with your own king guarding her. }) (1. Qd7 { A quiet move that keeps him boxed in, but it gives no check, so he strolls out of the corner and you start again. } 1... Kb8 2. Qb7# { Mate, but a move later than you needed. Your king is the reason he cannot eat your queen. }) (1. Qh1+ { A check from the far corner, and he answers it by stepping out of his own corner. Nothing at all has been trapped. } 1... Kb8 2. Qb7# { Mate in the end. Your queen touches his king and your king stands behind her, so taking her is not allowed. }) (1. Qf3+ { Another check from far away. His king has a free square beside him, so he simply uses it and lives. } 1... Kb8 2. Qb7# { Mate, one move after it was already on offer. Your king and queen finish him together in the corner. }) 1-0


