{ Last one: the queen kiss. Your queen lands right next to his king, with one of your own pieces guarding her, so he cannot take her. } 26... Qc6 { His queen slides across to go pawn hunting and leaves his king alone. Your rook guards the pawn next to his king, so take it with your queen. [%cal Yf5f7][%csl Gd7] } 27. Qxf7+ { Your queen is touching his king now, and your rook guards her along the row, so taking her is not allowed. [%cal Gd7f7] } (27. Rxf7 { The rook takes the pawn, but it is your queen who has to land beside his king. Now he gets time to defend. } 27... h6 28. Re7 Rf8 29. Qe5 Qf6 { The position is dead level, and the mate that was sitting there has gone for good. }) (27. Re7 { The rook grabs a fine looking square, but it does not touch his king, and your queen was the one with the mating job. } 27... Rf8 28. Qf4 c4 29. a5 Qd5 { Equal now, and his king has plenty of air around him. }) (27. Qxc5 { A free pawn, but your queen walks away from his king and the mate walks away with her. } 27... Qxc5 28. Rd1 Qb4 29. Ra1 { The queens have come off into a dead level ending, and the win has gone. }) (27. Rd8+ { A check, but his rook simply takes yours, and that rook was the one guarding the square next to his king. } 27... Rxd8 28. Qg4 Qf6 29. a5 { You are a whole rook down, and there is no mate anywhere on the board. }) 27... Kh8 { That corner is his only square. Now take the other pawn beside him: your rook covers the whole row behind her. [%cal Yf7g7,Gd7g7] } 28. Qxg7# { Mate! Your queen is right beside his king and your rook guards her from the far end of the row. That is the queen kiss. [%cal Gd7g7][%csl Rh8] } (28. Rd8+ { A check from the last row instead of the mate, and his rook just takes yours for nothing. } 28... Rxd8 29. Qc4 Re8 30. a5 { A whole rook gone, and the mate went with it. }) (28. Ra7 { Your rook was the only thing guarding your queen beside his king, and it wanders off after a pawn. } 28... Qf6 29. Qxf6 gxf6 { The queens come off and you are left with a dead equal ending. }) 1-0


